module
public import CFSG.Quasithin.Chapter15.Section03.CharacteristicTwo.RankOneConfiguration.Recognition.SmallExtension.MarkedMS.AlternatingSixReconstruction.Natural.Exhaustion.LargeDistance.SectionFive.PointThompson

/-!
# Original nonsylow radicals and their maximal replacement images

Let H be the original odd-index subgroup whose two-core is the MS2(a)
radical R. The minimum replacement D is characteristic in R, so H normalizes
D. In the replacement overgroup N, the subgroup H intersect N maps into the
normalizer of the literal replacement image in S₆. If that normalizer is a
two-group, then H intersect N is a two-group, since the quotient kernel is
the two-core. The quotient of the original group by N is also a two-group.
Thus H is a two-group; its odd index makes H = R a Sylow subgroup.

The normalizer condition holds for the mixed plane, the subgroup generated
by two disjoint transpositions, and every non-elementary two-subgroup of S₆. Conjugate into the canonical Sylow group:
all nontrivial squares are the same double transposition. Every normalizer
must centralize that element, whose centralizer lies in the canonical Sylow.
For either order-four plane, its unique double transposition gives the same bound.
The finite certificates use only six-letter coordinates and sixteen Sylow
words, without enumerating all permutations.

Consequently a nonsylow original radical has elementary replacement image.
In a point core the existing Thompson-image alternative is full or mixed;
the mixed alternative is now excluded. This supplies full point-core equality
and the distance bound four on the actual replacement. The exhaustive
elementary subgroup catalogue now eliminates every other proper elementary
image: the order-two groups are too small, the two-transposition and mixed
planes force the original radical to be Sylow, and the even plane cannot
generate S₆ together with the component A₆. The nonsylow branch therefore
has a full point core or the full group generated by three disjoint
transpositions, both of order eight. Beyond distance four only the latter
remains. Excluding that full three-pair image, the original Sylow branches
and full-point distance four remains with the large-distance assembly owner.
No radical is assumed Sylow, and both central covers and their attachments
are retained.

Source: Aschbacher--Smith B.3.2--4 and C.1.31 (MS2)(a), printed pp.83--85,135; Meierfrankenfeld--
Stellmacher, *Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank two* (1993), characteristic
replacement in Section 7, pp.932--933, and the Section 5 image analysis,
pp.913--927. This uses the original odd-index hypothesis in addition to the
replacement geometry; it does not assert the unconditional distance exclusion.
-/

public noncomputable section
